    "featured": true
  },
  "pages": [
    {
      "name": "intro",
      "elements": [
        {
          "type": "html",
          "name": "intro_overview",
          "html": "<h3>Purpose</h3><p>This survey asks Wyoming voters where they stand on four core policy areas: life, religious liberty, family values, and education freedom. Some of the policies voters prefer may be achievable through ordinary legislation. Others may require a constitutional amendment. The goal is to measure the depth and direction of public support — including support for constitutional change — before lawmakers act.</p><p><a class=\"button button--small button--secondary\" href=\"/assets/wy_family_four_pillars_sources_background.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Sources and background</a> &nbsp; <a class=\"button button--small button--secondary\" href=\"/assets/wy_family_four_pillars_constitutional_review_summary.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Constitutional context</a></p><h3>How responses will be used</h3><ul><li>Results will be published in aggregate.</li><li>Exact question wording and response options will be published with the results.</li><li>Verified and unverified responses may be reported separately.</li><li>Individual responses will remain confidential.</li></ul><h3>Four pillars</h3><p>This survey is organized into four sections: <strong>Life</strong> (Q1–8), <strong>Religious Liberty</strong> (Q9–16), <strong>Family Values</strong> (Q17–24), and <strong>Education Freedom</strong> (Q25–32). Each section ends with a question about whether a constitutional amendment is needed to achieve your preferred policy. You may complete all four sections or stop after any section.</p>"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "pillar_life",
      "title": "Pillar 1: Life",
      "description": "Questions 1–8 — abortion policy, exceptions, and constitutional threshold.",
      "elements": [
        {
          "type": "html",
          "name": "life_intro",
          "html": "<h3>Pillar 1: Life</h3><p>Wyoming has an active abortion statute and a constitutional provision at Article 1, Section 38 that protects health-care decisions. These questions measure where voters stand on the scope of any abortion restriction and whether a constitutional amendment would be needed to achieve their preferred policy.</p>"
        },
        {
          "type": "radiogroup",
          "name": "q1_life_protection_start",
          "title": "At what point should Wyoming law begin protecting a developing life?",
          "choices": [
            "At conception (fertilization)",
            "At detectable heartbeat or cardiac activity (around 6 weeks)",
            "At viability (when a fetus can survive outside the womb)",
            "At birth",
            "The law should not recognize a developing life as having legal protection before birth",
            "Not sure"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "radiogroup",
          "name": "q2_overall_abortion_policy",
          "title": "Which statement is closest to the abortion policy you want Wyoming law to have?",
          "choices": [
            "The law should generally allow abortion, with decisions left to the individual and clinician",
            "The law should allow abortion in many situations, with defined legal limits",
            "The law should allow abortion only in a few situations, with clear exceptions",
            "The law should generally prohibit abortion, with narrow exceptions",
            "Not sure"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "radiogroup",
          "name": "q3_timing_limits",
          "title": "If abortion is allowed, what timing limit should apply?",
          "choices": [
            "Up to 6 weeks",
            "Up to 12 weeks",
            "Up to 24 weeks",
            "No fixed limit — decisions should be based on medical judgment and circumstances",
            "Abortion should be allowed only in specific, narrow exceptions regardless of timing",
            "Not sure"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "checkbox",
          "name": "q4_exceptions",
          "title": "If abortion is generally restricted, which situations should qualify as a legal exception? (Select all that apply)",
          "choices": [
            "Life-threatening risk to the pregnant person",
            "Serious risk of major physical harm to the pregnant person",
            "Pregnancy resulting from rape",
            "Pregnancy resulting from incest",
            "Lethal fetal condition (fetus expected to die shortly after birth)",
            "Severe fetal anomaly likely to cause profound suffering",
            "Serious risk to mental health, diagnosed by a licensed clinician",
            "No exceptions — the restriction should apply in all cases",
            "Prefer not to answer"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "radiogroup",
          "name": "q5_physician_conscience",
          "title": "Should Wyoming law protect health care providers who decline to participate in abortion procedures based on conscience or religious belief?",
          "choices": [
            "Yes — all providers should be protected from compelled participation",
            "Yes — with a limit, such as requiring referral to a willing provider",
            "No — providers who accept public funding or hospital privileges should not be able to decline",
            "Not sure"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "radiogroup",
          "name": "q6_waiting_period_counseling",
          "title": "Should Wyoming law require a waiting period and written information before an abortion is performed?",
          "choices": [
            "Yes — a waiting period and written information should both be required",
            "Yes — written information should be required, but no waiting period",
            "No — these requirements create unnecessary barriers",
            "Not sure"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "radiogroup",
          "name": "q7_enforcement_approach",
          "title": "If Wyoming restricts abortion, who should have the primary role in enforcing those restrictions?",
          "choices": [
            "State law enforcement and prosecutors",
            "Civil penalties and lawsuits, not criminal charges",
            "Medical licensing boards",
            "A combination of these",
            "Not sure"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "radiogroup",
          "name": "q8_life_constitutional_amendment",
          "title": "Wyoming's Constitution (Article 1, Section 38) protects health-care decisions. If the abortion policy you prefer in Q1–7 cannot be achieved through ordinary legislation because of that provision, do you support adding a constitutional amendment to allow the Legislature to regulate or prohibit abortion?",
          "choices": [
            "Yes — I support a constitutional amendment if it is needed to achieve my preferred abortion policy",
            "No — I do not support a constitutional amendment on abortion",
            "I am not sure whether an amendment is needed",
            "I do not have a strong preference on abortion policy",
            "Not sure"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "comment",
          "name": "life_comment",
          "title": "Optional: Anything you would like to add about abortion policy or life protections?",
          "maxLength": 1200,
          "placeholder": "Optional"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "pillar_religious_liberty",
      "title": "Pillar 2: Religious Liberty",
      "description": "Questions 9–16 — religious freedom, conscience protections, and public funding for faith-based organizations.",
      "elements": [
        {
          "type": "html",
          "name": "religious_liberty_intro",
          "html": "<h3>Pillar 2: Religious Liberty</h3><p>Wyoming's Constitution (Article 1, Sections 18 and 19) protects religious freedom and prohibits public funds from going directly to a sectarian or religious society or institution. Most religious liberty changes can begin through ordinary statute, but some may require a constitutional amendment if voters want broader accommodation of faith-based participation in public programs.</p>"
        },
        {
          "type": "radiogroup",
          "name": "q9_government_neutrality",
          "title": "How should Wyoming government treat religious belief and practice compared to nonreligious activity?",
          "choices": [
            "Strictly neutral — government should neither favor nor burden religion",
            "Accommodating — government should make reasonable accommodations for religious practice",
            "Supportive — government should actively affirm the role of religion in public life",
            "Not sure"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "radiogroup",
          "name": "q10_conscience_exemptions",
          "title": "Should Wyoming law allow individuals and small-business owners to decline services or employment actions that conflict with their sincere religious beliefs?",
          "choices": [
            "Yes — broad conscience protections should apply to individuals and businesses",
            "Yes — but only for individuals, not businesses",
            "Yes — but only where no comparable alternative service is unavailable",
            "No — anti-discrimination law should apply regardless of religious belief",
            "Not sure"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "radiogroup",
          "name": "q11_faith_based_public_funding",
          "title": "Should Wyoming public funds be available to faith-based organizations that deliver social services (such as homeless shelters, addiction recovery programs, or foster care)?",
          "choices": [
            "Yes — on the same terms as nonreligious providers, with no religious-content restriction",
            "Yes — on the same terms, but faith-based programming must remain separate from the government-funded portion",
            "No — public funds should not go to organizations with religious affiliations",
            "Not sure"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "radiogroup",
          "name": "q12_religious_displays_public_property",
          "title": "Should Wyoming allow religious symbols or displays on government property (such as courthouses, parks, or public buildings)?",
          "choices": [
            "Yes — religious displays should be allowed alongside other cultural and historical displays",
            "Yes — but only if the display is historical or ceremonial, not devotional",
            "No — government property should remain religion-neutral",
            "Not sure"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "radiogroup",
          "name": "q13_prayer_government_settings",
          "title": "Should Wyoming explicitly protect voluntary, student-led prayer or religious expression in public schools and other government settings?",
          "choices": [
            "Yes — voluntary religious expression by students or participants should be explicitly protected in state law",
            "It is already protected — no new Wyoming law is needed",
            "No — the government setting makes any religious expression inappropriate",
            "Not sure"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "radiogroup",
          "name": "q14_religious_exemptions_from_laws",
          "title": "When a generally applicable Wyoming law conflicts with a person's sincere religious practice, what should happen?",
          "choices": [
            "The person should receive an exemption unless the government can show a compelling public-safety reason to deny it",
            "Exemptions should be available for churches and religious institutions, but not for individuals",
            "No exemptions — the same law should apply to everyone equally",
            "Not sure"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "radiogroup",
          "name": "q15_chaplains_public_institutions",
          "title": "Should Wyoming allow chaplains or religious counselors in government institutions such as prisons, hospitals, or public universities?",
          "choices": [
            "Yes — chaplains provide important support and should be allowed and publicly funded",
            "Yes — but chaplains should be privately funded, not paid with public funds",
            "No — government institutions should not employ or fund religious counselors",
            "Not sure"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "radiogroup",
          "name": "q16_religious_liberty_law_or_constitution",
          "title": "If Wyoming law currently prevents faith-based organizations from participating in public programs on the same terms as nonreligious organizations, do you support changing Wyoming law or the Wyoming Constitution to allow equal participation?",
          "choices": [
            "Yes — I support changing state law if needed to ensure equal access for faith-based organizations",
            "Yes — and I support a constitutional amendment if a statute alone is not enough",
            "No — the current limits on faith-based funding in the Wyoming Constitution should be preserved",
            "I am not sure whether current law actually prevents equal participation",
            "Not sure"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "comment",
          "name": "religious_liberty_comment",
          "title": "Optional: Anything you would like to add about religious liberty?",
          "maxLength": 1200,
          "placeholder": "Optional"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "pillar_family_values",
      "title": "Pillar 3: Family Values",
      "description": "Questions 17–24 — parental rights, minors' online access, and family-policy priorities.",
      "elements": [
        {
          "type": "html",
          "name": "family_values_intro",
          "html": "<h3>Pillar 3: Family Values</h3><p>Most family-values policies can begin through ordinary legislation or local school-board policy. A constitutional amendment becomes more likely if voters want an explicit parental-rights standard — one that creates a higher legal bar for government intervention in family decisions than ordinary statutes provide.</p>"
        },
        {
          "type": "radiogroup",
          "name": "q17_parental_consent_medical",
          "title": "Should Wyoming law require parental notification and consent before a minor receives non-emergency medical treatment?",
          "choices": [
            "Yes — parents should have the right to consent or refuse all non-emergency medical treatment for minors",
            "Yes — with an exception when a court finds a parent is acting against the child's best interest",
            "No — older minors should have the right to make certain medical decisions independently",
            "Not sure"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "radiogroup",
          "name": "q18_parental_school_access",
          "title": "Should Wyoming law give parents the explicit right to review all curriculum materials, instructional content, and school records for their minor children?",
          "choices": [
            "Yes — parents should have full access to all materials and records",
            "Yes — with reasonable time and process limits to protect school operations",
            "No — existing federal law (FERPA) already provides sufficient access rights",
            "Not sure"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "radiogroup",
          "name": "q19_parental_rights_standard",
          "title": "Should Wyoming establish a strong legal standard recognizing that parents have the primary right to direct the upbringing, education, and care of their children — requiring the government to meet a high bar before overriding that right?",
          "choices": [
            "Yes — and this standard should be written into Wyoming law as a statute",
            "Yes — and this standard should be written into the Wyoming Constitution",
            "The current legal standard is sufficient",
            "No — a higher parental-rights standard could interfere with child-welfare protections",
            "Not sure"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "radiogroup",
          "name": "q20_gender_social_transition_schools",
          "title": "Should Wyoming law require parental notification and consent before a school employee or official takes action to socially transition a student to a different name, pronoun, or gender identity?",
          "choices": [
            "Yes — parents must be notified and give consent before any school-initiated social transition",
            "Yes — with an exception for students who report a safety risk at home",
            "No — student privacy and safety may outweigh mandatory parental notification in some cases",
            "Not sure"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "radiogroup",
          "name": "q21_minors_online_access",
          "title": "Should Wyoming law require online platforms and social media companies to verify user age and restrict minors' access to harmful content?",
          "choices": [
            "Yes — Wyoming should require age verification and content restrictions for minors",
            "Yes — but parental consent and control tools are a better approach than government mandates",
            "No — age verification raises privacy concerns and this is better handled at the federal level",
            "Not sure"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "radiogroup",
          "name": "q22_parental_opt_out_instruction",
          "title": "Should Wyoming law require public schools to allow parents to opt their minor children out of instruction that conflicts with the family's religious or moral beliefs?",
          "choices": [
            "Yes — parents should have a clear opt-out right for instruction on religious or moral grounds",
            "Yes — but only for specific topics such as sex education or gender identity",
            "No — uniform curriculum is important and opt-out decisions should remain with school boards",
            "Not sure"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "radiogroup",
          "name": "q23_parental_vs_state_authority",
          "title": "When the state believes a child's welfare is at risk, what standard should apply before the government can override a parent's medical, educational, or custody decision?",
          "choices": [
            "Government should be required to show clear and convincing evidence of serious danger before overriding a parent",
            "The current best-interest-of-the-child standard is appropriate",
            "Government should have broad authority to protect children when a serious risk is identified",
            "Not sure"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "radiogroup",
          "name": "q24_who_drives_family_policy",
          "title": "Who do you believe should primarily drive family-values policy changes in Wyoming?",
          "choices": [
            "Parents and families",
            "Local school boards and educators",
            "The Wyoming Legislature",
            "Courts interpreting existing law",
            "A balanced process involving all of these",
            "Not sure"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "comment",
          "name": "family_values_comment",
          "title": "Optional: Anything you would like to add about parental rights or family policy?",
          "maxLength": 1200,
          "placeholder": "Optional"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "pillar_education_freedom",
      "title": "Pillar 4: Education Freedom",
      "description": "Questions 25–32 — school choice, public funding for nonpublic options, accountability, and constitutional threshold.",
      "elements": [
        {
          "type": "html",
          "name": "education_freedom_intro",
          "html": "<h3>Pillar 4: Education Freedom</h3><p>Wyoming's Constitution (Article 7, Section 8) prohibits public school funds from going to support or assist any private school, religious school, or institution controlled by a church or religious organization. Broad school choice funding — including education savings accounts, vouchers, or tuition payments to private or religious schools — most likely requires a constitutional amendment before the Legislature can enact it.</p>"
        },
        {
          "type": "radiogroup",
          "name": "q25_school_choice_overall",
          "title": "Overall, do you support giving Wyoming families more options for how to use their children's share of public education funding?",
          "choices": [
            "Yes — families should have broad freedom to choose the best education option for their child",
            "Yes — with limits to protect public school funding levels and accountability",
            "No — public education funding should remain in the public school system",
            "Not sure"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "radiogroup",
          "name": "q26_public_funds_private_schools",
          "title": "Should Wyoming public education funding follow the student to a private or charter school when a family chooses that option?",
          "choices": [
            "Yes — funding should follow the student to any accredited school the family chooses",
            "Yes — but only to charter schools, not private or religious schools",
            "No — public funds should remain in public schools",
            "Not sure"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "radiogroup",
          "name": "q27_accountability_nonpublic",
          "title": "If private or religious schools receive public funds, what accountability should they face?",
          "choices": [
            "The same academic standards and transparency requirements as public schools",
            "Basic financial accountability and student outcome reporting, but not full public-school requirements",
            "Minimal accountability — families choosing the school provide sufficient oversight",
            "Not sure"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "radiogroup",
          "name": "q28_homeschool_funding",
          "title": "Should Wyoming provide funding support (such as tax credits, education savings accounts, or direct stipends) to families who homeschool their children?",
          "choices": [
            "Yes — homeschooling families should receive the same per-pupil support as families using other options",
            "Yes — but only for approved curriculum and materials, not a general per-pupil payment",
            "No — families who choose to homeschool should not receive public education funds",
            "Not sure"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "radiogroup",
          "name": "q29_transparency_reporting",
          "title": "If public funds are used for nonpublic education options, should Wyoming require public reporting of how those funds are spent and what student outcomes result?",
          "choices": [
            "Yes — full public reporting comparable to what public schools provide",
            "Yes — basic financial and outcome reporting, but not full public-school disclosure",
            "No — detailed reporting requirements reduce the flexibility that makes school choice valuable",
            "Not sure"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "radiogroup",
          "name": "q30_education_savings_accounts",
          "title": "Should Wyoming create an Education Savings Account (ESA) or similar program that allows families to use a portion of per-pupil funding for approved education expenses — including private school tuition, tutoring, curriculum, or therapies?",
          "choices": [
            "Yes — a broad ESA open to all Wyoming families",
            "Yes — but targeted to lower-income families or students with special needs",
            "No — public funds should not be used for private education expenses",
            "Not sure"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "radiogroup",
          "name": "q31_special_education_portability",
          "title": "Should Wyoming allow families of students with disabilities to take their child's special education funding to a private or nonpublic setting if they believe it better serves their child's needs?",
          "choices": [
            "Yes — special education funding should be portable to any setting that meets the child's needs",
            "Yes — but only to programs meeting the same standards as public special education services",
            "No — special education services and funding should remain within the public school system",
            "Not sure"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "radiogroup",
          "name": "q32_education_constitutional_amendment",
          "title": "Wyoming's Constitution currently prohibits public education funds from supporting private or religious schools. If the school choice policies you prefer in Q25–31 require a constitutional amendment to enact, do you support adding one?",
          "choices": [
            "Yes — I support a constitutional amendment to allow public education funds to follow students to private schools, religious schools, homeschool programs, or other approved options",
            "No — I do not support a constitutional amendment to expand school choice funding",
            "I am not sure whether an amendment is needed for the policies I prefer",
            "I do not have a strong preference on school choice funding",
            "Not sure"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "comment",
          "name": "education_freedom_comment",
          "title": "Optional: Anything you would like to add about education funding or school choice?",
          "maxLength": 1200,
          "placeholder": "Optional"
        }
      ]
    },
